WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.334
You have to be persistent.

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.920
You have to, you know,
listen to all the no's,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.672
which we got many, many of

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.465
and then just keep pushing forward.

00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:09.968
Bring as much passion
as you can to something,

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:12.053
lead with your heart,
build the right team,

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:13.722
the right management team,

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:14.973
and then just build upon that

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:15.807
and it starts to snowball.

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:17.434
And that's really what
we did at Beyond Meat.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.947
Well, look, I'm honored to have you here

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:32.282
and I must start off by saying

00:00:32.282 --> 00:00:33.992
that I'm a proud investor

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:35.535
and I have to let you know, Ethan,

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:38.913
that you have a lot to do

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:41.124
with why I went plant-based to begin.

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:43.501
You one of the first
examples I always go to

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:45.837
when talking about the
impact this can have

00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:46.671
on someone's life

00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:49.966
and just you as role model for kids.

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:52.969
It's been amazing, even
aside from Beyond Meat,

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:54.804
the carbon footprint and everything.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:57.599
Now getting onto some
of the questions Ethan.

00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:59.309
So you started off in clean tech.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:01.186
I did.
How and why?

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:02.729
Yeah, so when I was younger

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.981
and I had love a for basketball,

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:06.983
but not the same talent as you do

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:08.693
so we had to go do something else.

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:11.738
And I was sitting in my dad's office

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:15.950
and I was talking about kind
of what I should be doing

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.452
as I thought about my career.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:18.578
You know, he asked me a question.

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:21.706
He asked me, "What is the
biggest problem in the world

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:22.707
as you see it?"

00:01:22.707 --> 00:01:24.542
And I thought about that for a long time.

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:27.587
And to me, it really came
back as being climate.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.840
And so the correlate to
that question was, you know,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.008
then what do you do about it?

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:34.344
And at the time,

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:37.097
the thinking was really
around alternative energy.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:41.017
So looking at ways to
migrate from fossil fuels to

00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:43.812
renewable and cleaner sources of energy.

00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:46.898
And there was kind of a basket
of different technologies,

00:01:46.898 --> 00:01:50.068
whether it was lithium ion
batteries, solar cells,

00:01:50.068 --> 00:01:54.781
wind, fuel cells, and I began
working on a number of those

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:55.824
as a kind of analyst

00:01:55.824 --> 00:01:59.786
and then joined a company
that was focused on

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:02.163
hydrogen powered, specifically,

00:02:02.163 --> 00:02:04.124
proton exchange membrane fuel cells.

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:07.210
And I loved it. I loved
working in that space.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.420
But I kept feeling that
I was missing something.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.132
And that led me to the
work that I'm doing now.

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:14.634
How does clean tech get you to food?

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:15.760
Like how did that happen?

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.594
I mean, first of all,

00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:20.515
what we're talking about
in clean energy is fuel

00:02:20.515 --> 00:02:24.269
to move things around, to warm buildings,

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:28.356
to provide the electricity
that we so enjoy,

00:02:28.356 --> 00:02:29.941
and things of that nature.

00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:32.485
What we're talking about in
food is fuel for our bodies.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:34.863
And so there are some
common elements there,

00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:35.864
but more importantly,

00:02:35.864 --> 00:02:37.198
it was through problem solving.

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:39.576
I began to really think more about climate

00:02:39.576 --> 00:02:41.327
in maybe a more
sophisticated way than I had

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.037
when I was just a student

00:02:43.037 --> 00:02:46.457
and started to think about
the impact that livestock

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:47.584
has on climate.

00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:52.589
And to me, just the sheer
amount of land, energy, water,

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:55.341
and emissions that are involved

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:58.178
in bringing a piece of
protein to the plate

00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:00.638
from an animal that was so significant

00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:03.433
that I felt I had to go
do something about it.

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:07.604
It's one thing to know
all of those working parts

00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:09.981
and all the different intricacies.

00:03:10.815 --> 00:03:12.775
Like me, once I started
eating healthy, right,

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:15.236
I started trying to figure
out what foods to eat.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.284
But you got Beyond Meat, right?

00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:24.037
Like what inspired you, right.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:27.040
If you think about what meat is,

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.293
and this is really where I
think things get exciting.

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:34.255
Meat is a composition that
is very familiar to us

00:03:34.255 --> 00:03:35.757
and it's essentially five things

00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:37.717
if you think about it
at a really high level.

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.178
It's amino acid, it's
lipids, it's trace minerals,

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:41.554
it's vitamins, and it's water.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:44.849
Said more simply, it's basically
protein, fat, and water.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:46.017
And what the animal's doing is

00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:48.144
they're consuming a large
amount of vegetation.

00:03:48.144 --> 00:03:49.854
You know, you look at Bovine,

00:03:49.854 --> 00:03:53.441
they're eating grass or being fed feed.

00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:55.235
And they're converting that plant material

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:56.569
through their digestive tract

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.488
and their skeletal muscular system

00:03:58.488 --> 00:04:02.325
into a center of the plate
protein, into muscle, right.

00:04:02.325 --> 00:04:04.369
And we harvest that muscle
as we slaughter the animal,

00:04:04.369 --> 00:04:05.203
pull it out,

00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:06.829
and then we prepare that as something

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:08.289
we put at the center of our plate.

00:04:08.289 --> 00:04:10.124
Well, the tremendous news here,

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:13.920
and this has to do with
the evolution of the earth

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.964
and our species within it,

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:18.258
is that there's a commonality in a unity

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:19.884
between plants and animals,

00:04:19.884 --> 00:04:22.553
in terms of the core material of parts.

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:24.138
So what you find in plants,

00:04:24.138 --> 00:04:27.892
you also find in human and animal bodies

00:04:27.892 --> 00:04:29.185
just presenting differently,

00:04:29.185 --> 00:04:31.271
but it's that combination of proteins

00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:34.565
and fats and water that you
can pull those from plants,

00:04:34.565 --> 00:04:36.109
or you can get them from animals.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:38.278
And our idea was to just,

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:41.489
not to make a soybean behave like a steak,

00:04:41.489 --> 00:04:44.951
but rather let's find the
core parts of meat in plants.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:46.286
Let's pull them from the plants

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:48.705
and let's assemble them
against the architecture

00:04:48.705 --> 00:04:51.291
or blueprint of animal muscle or meat.

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:52.959
What was your advantage, right?

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.837
And how did you get
investors to believe in you?

00:04:55.837 --> 00:04:58.172
Right, cause I'm sure when
you came to some people,

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:00.717
they were like, what is he talking about?

00:05:00.717 --> 00:05:02.927
Like we not trying to do this.

00:05:02.927 --> 00:05:05.096
We like to eat our meat.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:05.888
We like this stuff.

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:06.723
What is he talking about?

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:08.975
How did you get investors to believe

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.727
in what you were building?

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:12.270
I mean, it's very similar

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.771
and this is why I love sports so much,

00:05:13.771 --> 00:05:17.066
to what I've seen you go through.

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:17.900
And I obviously,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.485
I know so much about your career as a fan

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:23.364
and the persistence that you had to show.

00:05:23.364 --> 00:05:27.493
And, you know, transiting
from football to basketball.

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.995
So I think that the biggest thing,

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:30.580
you know, when I was in business school,

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:33.291
people would talk about, oh,
you need a competitive moat.

00:05:33.291 --> 00:05:36.085
And generally they would say
that has to be some advantage

00:05:36.085 --> 00:05:39.213
from a technology perspective
going into a sector

00:05:39.213 --> 00:05:41.382
so that you can create
a competitive advantage

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:44.052
that someone else can't knock out.

00:05:44.052 --> 00:05:46.429
And unfortunately, that's not really true.

00:05:46.429 --> 00:05:49.307
What you do need is to
work harder than anybody,

00:05:49.307 --> 00:05:53.061
to be smart, and surround
yourself with very smart people.

00:05:53.061 --> 00:05:55.396
But it's not like I went into this space

00:05:55.396 --> 00:05:57.398
with infallible technology.

00:05:57.398 --> 00:05:59.692
I went in with a very good technology

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:02.612
and one that was very good
at creating muscle structure,

00:06:02.612 --> 00:06:04.697
but it was what we've done after that,

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:07.450
that really has helped propel the company.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.452
And so you have to be persistent.

00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:11.454
You have to, you know,

00:06:11.454 --> 00:06:12.955
listen to all the no's,

00:06:12.955 --> 00:06:14.749
which we got many, many of,

00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:16.876
and then just keep pushing forward.

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:20.046
But bring as much passion
as you can to something,

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:22.382
lead with your heart,
build the right team,

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:25.343
the right management team,
have good technology,

00:06:25.343 --> 00:06:26.552
and then just build upon that

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:27.387
and it starts to snowball.

00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:29.305
And that's really what
we did at Beyond Meat.

00:06:29.305 --> 00:06:31.808
What was it like for you though

00:06:31.808 --> 00:06:33.434
when you were getting those no's

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:38.439
and then obviously Beyond
Meat is very successful now.

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:40.691
Like, do you remember a lot of those no's

00:06:40.691 --> 00:06:43.027
and it's okay to have a
chip on your shoulder?

00:06:45.321 --> 00:06:46.280
Well, I love that.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:49.826
It's funny, I just was texting
one of my children about,

00:06:49.826 --> 00:06:51.244
they were going into practice.

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:53.162
I said, make sure you do play
with a chip on your shoulder

00:06:53.162 --> 00:06:54.789
because you know,

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:57.542
you have to find some ways
to feel yourself every day.

00:06:57.542 --> 00:06:59.377
And so I certainly do have that.

00:06:59.377 --> 00:07:01.754
And in this room here,
there are quotes on the wall

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.714
from people who have doubted us.

00:07:03.714 --> 00:07:04.674
You know, people in media

00:07:04.674 --> 00:07:07.635
and things like that saying
that this can't be done.

00:07:07.635 --> 00:07:09.887
And I use those a lot to motivate myself

00:07:09.887 --> 00:07:11.305
and to motivate my team.

00:07:11.305 --> 00:07:15.059
And so, yeah, I remember
all of 'em actually.

00:07:15.059 --> 00:07:17.520
Where, you know, you're
sitting across from a table,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.522
you really need someone to invest

00:07:19.522 --> 00:07:20.356
and believe in you.

00:07:20.356 --> 00:07:21.441
And, you know, they turn you down

00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:22.900
and they turn you down again and again.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.736
And you know, it's persistence

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:29.282
and it's a commitment to the
product at the end of the day.

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:31.409
Like we have to keep making
the product better and better.

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:33.494
And we have this program at Beyond Meat,

00:07:33.494 --> 00:07:35.997
that's called Beyond Meat Rapid
and Relentless Innovation.

00:07:35.997 --> 00:07:37.582
And to talk about sports again,

00:07:37.582 --> 00:07:40.293
actually stole that word,
that use of that word,

00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:42.462
from the warriors back when they were,

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:44.714
you know, just, I went to
a conference there one day

00:07:44.714 --> 00:07:46.174
and they kept using that word.

00:07:46.174 --> 00:07:47.592
I said, "Well, that's
really what I want this team

00:07:47.592 --> 00:07:49.927
to be doing within our
labs is just be relentless

00:07:49.927 --> 00:07:52.180
on being the best out there.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.724
At taking protein fats,

00:07:54.724 --> 00:07:56.350
minerals, vitamins, water

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:57.393
from non animal sources

00:07:57.393 --> 00:08:00.313
and building them into
the structure of meat

00:08:00.313 --> 00:08:01.856
and let's get better and better every day.

00:08:01.856 --> 00:08:03.983
And that's the only way
to prove people wrong."

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:05.651
And by the way, with
these deals we just did

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:08.321
with McDonald's, KFC,
Pizza Hut, and Taco Bell.

00:08:10.364 --> 00:08:12.783
Our promise to them is
that we will out compete

00:08:12.783 --> 00:08:14.035
the other people in the market.

00:08:14.035 --> 00:08:16.245
Like we will just bring
you the best product.

00:08:16.245 --> 00:08:20.374
And that's how we have
to build our reputation

00:08:20.374 --> 00:08:22.460
and how we have to build
our company is not through,

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:23.711
you can try to get contracts

00:08:23.711 --> 00:08:25.004
where you force people
to take your products,

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:26.172
or what have you.

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:27.006
Better way to live

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:28.424
and the better way to do things is just

00:08:28.424 --> 00:08:30.009
always produce the best product.

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:32.845
And so, you know, as I
was getting those no's,

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.597
I just went home and
made the product better.

00:08:34.597 --> 00:08:35.431
Just went, you know,

00:08:35.431 --> 00:08:36.766
worked with my team to
make the product better

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:37.600
and better and better.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.061
And once you do that, at that point,

00:08:40.061 --> 00:08:41.437
you start to see customers come to you.

00:08:41.437 --> 00:08:43.898
You start to see investors come to you.

00:08:43.898 --> 00:08:46.108
Being in athletics my entire life,

00:08:46.108 --> 00:08:48.194
playing football, playing basketball,

00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:49.779
and especially when I made it to the NBA,

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:52.156
everyone used to always ask the question,

00:08:52.156 --> 00:08:55.117
when did you know that you
were gonna make it, right?

00:08:55.117 --> 00:08:57.036
Like, you have some prodigies

00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:58.079
that know they're gonna make it

00:08:58.079 --> 00:08:59.372
when they're in middle school now.

00:08:59.372 --> 00:09:00.873
And I always say for me,

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:05.127
I didn't know until probably
my sophomore year in college.

00:09:05.127 --> 00:09:08.089
That's when I really was like, I'm ready.

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:09.757
I'm ready. I can go to the NBA.

00:09:09.757 --> 00:09:11.384
I know I'll be ready.

00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:14.136
When did you know that
you were onto something?

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:17.139
So I have this thing where

00:09:17.139 --> 00:09:19.016
I never thought the business would fail.

00:09:19.016 --> 00:09:20.893
I didn't know how it
would come out though.

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:22.728
You know, I just didn't.

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:23.646
You know, we talked about

00:09:23.646 --> 00:09:25.147
how do you get through the no's and stuff,

00:09:25.147 --> 00:09:28.150
and one of the ways is to

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:30.278
cut your safety net completely, right.

00:09:30.278 --> 00:09:32.989
And I didn't necessarily
do that consciously,

00:09:32.989 --> 00:09:34.407
but I did it anyway.

00:09:34.407 --> 00:09:36.409
And what I mean by that is,

00:09:36.409 --> 00:09:37.743
you know, I had a very good job

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:41.455
and had, you know, some savings

00:09:41.455 --> 00:09:42.748
and home and all this stuff.

00:09:42.748 --> 00:09:45.585
But, you know, you say, I'm
never gonna touch those savings,

00:09:45.585 --> 00:09:47.295
but you do and you burn through them.

00:09:47.295 --> 00:09:48.629
And then, you know,

00:09:48.629 --> 00:09:50.715
I'm not gonna sell that
house, but you do, you know?

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:55.052
And so then it's not so much
bravery that keeps you going,

00:09:55.052 --> 00:09:56.804
it's just you got to.

00:09:56.804 --> 00:09:58.556
I'm sharing that because, you know,

00:09:58.556 --> 00:10:03.019
even today you have to
have a healthy amount of,

00:10:04.228 --> 00:10:05.146
I dunno if it's fear

00:10:05.146 --> 00:10:08.608
or a belief that you
need to keep pushing hard

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:09.609
to be successful.

00:10:09.609 --> 00:10:11.193
That you can't rest, right.

00:10:11.193 --> 00:10:14.071
And so one of the things I love about,

00:10:14.071 --> 00:10:17.283
particularly college coaches
and even NBA coaches is,

00:10:17.283 --> 00:10:18.909
how many times you see a
NBA coach, like, you know,

00:10:18.909 --> 00:10:22.288
smiling and dancing up
and down when they win.

00:10:22.288 --> 00:10:23.539
They look relieved.

00:10:23.539 --> 00:10:25.207
Like, if you look at like
Coach K when he wins,

00:10:25.207 --> 00:10:27.084
he just looks relieved.

00:10:27.084 --> 00:10:27.918
Right.
You know.

00:10:27.918 --> 00:10:29.378
And that's sort of the sense of what you,

00:10:29.378 --> 00:10:30.963
when you have a business like this

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:32.923
or a career like yours,

00:10:32.923 --> 00:10:35.843
you know, oftentimes it's
just a relief to have won.

00:10:35.843 --> 00:10:39.513
And I feel that way as
we're growing our business.

00:10:39.513 --> 00:10:42.058
And so I would say the
moment that I felt like,

00:10:42.058 --> 00:10:44.268
my God, this is actually turning

00:10:44.268 --> 00:10:47.313
and this is something that
could have the global impact,

00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:50.399
when we started getting these
large quick serve restaurants.

00:10:50.399 --> 00:10:51.359
And so, you know,

00:10:51.359 --> 00:10:53.235
one of the first was A&amp;W

00:10:54.487 --> 00:10:55.821
and then here in Southern California,

00:10:55.821 --> 00:10:58.115
Carl's and Del Taco,

00:10:58.115 --> 00:11:00.534
but then in Canada McDonald's.

00:11:00.534 --> 00:11:01.786
And I went there myself,

00:11:01.786 --> 00:11:05.665
I was in Toronto and they
did the test in a rural area.

00:11:05.665 --> 00:11:07.291
And I got in a car late at night,

00:11:07.291 --> 00:11:08.459
and I drove out there

00:11:08.459 --> 00:11:10.961
and I sat down in the
restaurant and I had it.

00:11:10.961 --> 00:11:13.381
And I got that feeling of like, you know,

00:11:13.381 --> 00:11:14.674
really can't believe this is happening.

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:16.175
And that was a blessing for me.

00:11:16.175 --> 00:11:18.761
And a wonderful feeling.

00:11:18.761 --> 00:11:21.847
You created a product that
didn't exist before, right.

00:11:21.847 --> 00:11:23.557
So you're an inventor.

00:11:23.557 --> 00:11:26.060
Like, what was, you laughing about it,

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:27.103
but that's the truth.

00:11:27.103 --> 00:11:30.314
Like, sometimes people will
come out with something

00:11:30.314 --> 00:11:31.482
that already exists

00:11:31.482 --> 00:11:33.734
and just try to make a
better version of it.

00:11:33.734 --> 00:11:35.611
You really invented something.

00:11:35.611 --> 00:11:38.197
So what was your biggest obstacle?

00:11:39.073 --> 00:11:40.324
Yeah, there were a lot.

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:42.785
The first obstacle was myself.

00:11:42.785 --> 00:11:47.373
You know, was just convincing myself that

00:11:48.457 --> 00:11:49.917
well, answering a calling, basically.

00:11:49.917 --> 00:11:51.877
You know, if I didn't do this,

00:11:51.877 --> 00:11:53.796
would I be happy in life, you know?

00:11:53.796 --> 00:11:56.924
And it takes a lot to get yourself out of,

00:11:56.924 --> 00:11:59.802
you know, a routine where
you're paying for your mortgage,

00:11:59.802 --> 00:12:01.470
you're saving money, and all that stuff,

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:05.015
to do something that is
outside of your field.

00:12:05.015 --> 00:12:05.891
And so for me,

00:12:05.891 --> 00:12:07.852
it just a discomfort started to build,

00:12:07.852 --> 00:12:08.894
I was like, you know,

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:10.271
said, I really have to go do this

00:12:10.271 --> 00:12:12.064
and satisfy this issue.

00:12:12.064 --> 00:12:13.607
So that was the first obstacle

00:12:13.607 --> 00:12:15.443
was getting over my own

00:12:15.443 --> 00:12:17.945
sense of trepidation about it.

00:12:17.945 --> 00:12:18.946
And then second,

00:12:20.156 --> 00:12:24.785
you know, was just this
constant need to overcome,

00:12:26.787 --> 00:12:27.997
there's a cultural barrier to this

00:12:27.997 --> 00:12:30.207
that still exists today.

00:12:30.207 --> 00:12:31.083
That, you know,

00:12:31.083 --> 00:12:32.710
I think one of the reasons that

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:34.503
I find your work with us

00:12:34.503 --> 00:12:37.590
and your work overall on this subject,

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:39.091
so incredibly compelling is

00:12:39.091 --> 00:12:42.470
we have to displace, attack head on,

00:12:43.429 --> 00:12:44.972
and really something that's a-scientific.

00:12:44.972 --> 00:12:47.308
And that is that animal protein

00:12:47.308 --> 00:12:49.935
is better for you in terms of athleticism

00:12:49.935 --> 00:12:52.229
and everything else than planet protein.

00:12:52.229 --> 00:12:54.607
What's true is that a complete set of

00:12:54.607 --> 00:12:56.609
amino acid is really good for you, right,

00:12:56.609 --> 00:12:57.485
as an athlete.

00:12:57.485 --> 00:12:59.361
And healthy, clean fats
are really good for you.

00:12:59.361 --> 00:13:01.530
We can take all those from plants

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.158
and build them into the structure of meat

00:13:04.158 --> 00:13:05.659
so that you can satisfy

00:13:05.659 --> 00:13:08.162
and grow your body and

00:13:08.162 --> 00:13:10.456
perform at your highest level.

00:13:10.456 --> 00:13:11.957
But yet there's a cultural stereotype

00:13:11.957 --> 00:13:13.542
that's the exact opposite, you know.

00:13:13.542 --> 00:13:14.919
And so, this ideas that-
Right.

00:13:14.919 --> 00:13:16.837
To be a robust athlete

00:13:16.837 --> 00:13:19.340
and to be a masculine presence

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:20.674
that you need to have animal protein,

00:13:20.674 --> 00:13:21.801
and it's just a-scientific.

00:13:21.801 --> 00:13:23.969
And so we had to get through that,

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:25.846
and we're still trying
to get through that.

00:13:25.846 --> 00:13:27.890
And because the irony is that

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:30.226
if you do wanna perform
at the highest level,

00:13:30.226 --> 00:13:31.936
cleaning your body of some of the things

00:13:31.936 --> 00:13:34.522
that are in animal
protein is the way to go.

00:13:34.522 --> 00:13:37.316
And so we're just learning
so much about the role

00:13:37.316 --> 00:13:38.150
that food can play,

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:39.527
whether it's in, you know,

00:13:39.527 --> 00:13:41.445
the inflammation you feel in your knees

00:13:41.445 --> 00:13:44.990
or potential precursor
or contributor rather

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:48.327
to Alzheimer's disease, to heart disease.

00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:50.788
Inflammation is the root
of so many of the things

00:13:50.788 --> 00:13:53.916
that are causing these
diseases that are so horrific.

00:13:53.916 --> 00:13:56.126
And we can influence those
through the food we eat,

00:13:56.126 --> 00:13:57.962
through how it conditions our microbiome

00:13:57.962 --> 00:13:59.755
and the bacteria in our gut.

00:13:59.755 --> 00:14:02.049
And we can play a role
at that at Beyond Meat.

00:14:03.133 --> 00:14:04.134
Trust me, Ethan,

00:14:04.134 --> 00:14:06.428
everything you saying right now is,

00:14:06.428 --> 00:14:08.472
I told you it's been life changing for me.

00:14:08.472 --> 00:14:11.267
And I think what you're
saying is so important,

00:14:11.267 --> 00:14:14.228
'cause it's the education component of it.

00:14:14.228 --> 00:14:17.648
And I think that's what
so people lack in general,

00:14:17.648 --> 00:14:21.026
but especially the African
American community.

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:24.780
My aunts and uncles, my dad,

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:27.908
I think for me, this change in lifestyle,

00:14:27.908 --> 00:14:29.451
as far as diet and eating it,

00:14:30.703 --> 00:14:32.621
it really hit me hard

00:14:32.621 --> 00:14:34.748
and it started off performance based,

00:14:34.748 --> 00:14:37.877
but then it became life based, right?

00:14:37.877 --> 00:14:40.921
And if no one else ever listens to me

00:14:40.921 --> 00:14:43.549
when I get on one of my rants

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:45.134
and talking about how to eat healthy

00:14:45.134 --> 00:14:47.011
and what it does for your gut,

00:14:47.011 --> 00:14:50.806
my dad turned 60 this
past year and you know,

00:14:50.806 --> 00:14:52.224
I was driving on the highway

00:14:52.224 --> 00:14:55.060
and my dad called me and
he called me on FaceTime

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:56.437
and he was in tears.

00:14:56.437 --> 00:14:58.439
And he was just telling me
that he had went to the doctor

00:14:58.439 --> 00:15:00.399
and the doctor has told
him that his health

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:02.902
was so much better, was so much better.

00:15:02.902 --> 00:15:05.779
So, I thank you for educating me

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:08.991
to begin with so that I can
try to educate those around me.

00:15:08.991 --> 00:15:12.036
So off of that moment right there,

00:15:12.036 --> 00:15:13.495
so one of the things too,

00:15:14.872 --> 00:15:16.957
that obviously people know is that

00:15:16.957 --> 00:15:20.878
Beyond Meat is still more expensive than

00:15:20.878 --> 00:15:22.046
regular meat, right?

00:15:22.046 --> 00:15:24.131
And I know that you said
that that will flip.

00:15:24.131 --> 00:15:27.176
Like, when and what will it
take to make that happen?

00:15:27.176 --> 00:15:29.553
Yeah, so we set a goal two years ago

00:15:29.553 --> 00:15:32.181
to within five years be able
to underprice animal protein

00:15:32.181 --> 00:15:34.350
in at least one category.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:36.226
And that's so important

00:15:36.226 --> 00:15:39.063
because right now we're asking
the question of the consumer,

00:15:39.063 --> 00:15:41.440
let's say in a quick serve restaurant

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.026
you know, do they want to transition

00:15:44.026 --> 00:15:47.863
or substitute a plant-based
meat into their diet.

00:15:48.906 --> 00:15:50.950
But also do they wanna pay more for it?

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:52.451
And we gotta get a away
from that second question.

00:15:52.451 --> 00:15:53.744
That second question is not helpful.

00:15:53.744 --> 00:15:56.497
And what's terrific
about what we're doing,

00:15:56.497 --> 00:15:59.041
you know, if you believe in
this model of efficiency,

00:15:59.041 --> 00:16:00.042
we talk about the animal

00:16:00.042 --> 00:16:02.002
being the biggest
bottleneck in agriculture.

00:16:02.002 --> 00:16:04.838
So we have increased the
efficiency of our fields

00:16:04.838 --> 00:16:06.632
over the last 100 years,

00:16:06.632 --> 00:16:08.258
an incredible amount, right?

00:16:08.258 --> 00:16:09.760
So, you know, corn, soy, and wheat,

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:14.515
we're growing hugely
more quantities per acre

00:16:14.515 --> 00:16:16.809
than ever before in human history.

00:16:18.102 --> 00:16:19.228
But what are we doing with that?

00:16:19.228 --> 00:16:21.105
We're running it through the same system

00:16:21.105 --> 00:16:24.024
to convert it into meat,
which is the animal.

00:16:24.024 --> 00:16:26.443
And the animal's largely
standing still in the sense that,

00:16:26.443 --> 00:16:28.696
the animal's not evolving,

00:16:28.696 --> 00:16:30.906
at a pace that we can see anyway,

00:16:30.906 --> 00:16:33.117
into a more efficient bioreactor.

00:16:33.117 --> 00:16:34.785
So if we remove the animal

00:16:34.785 --> 00:16:36.245
and we put in this technology,

00:16:36.245 --> 00:16:37.496
which takes two to three minutes

00:16:37.496 --> 00:16:40.249
to create that muscle like structure,

00:16:40.249 --> 00:16:43.168
we should become less expensive.

00:16:43.168 --> 00:16:45.087
We have tremendous efficiency advantages

00:16:45.087 --> 00:16:48.090
over animal protein.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:49.717
And we had University of Michigan

00:16:49.717 --> 00:16:50.759
do this great study where we, you know,

00:16:50.759 --> 00:16:54.179
we use 99%, less water, 93% less land.

00:16:54.179 --> 00:16:55.389
So why are we more expensive?

00:16:55.389 --> 00:16:56.348
It doesn't make sense.

00:16:56.348 --> 00:16:58.017
The inputs are, you know, plants.

00:16:58.017 --> 00:16:59.143
They're things like peas and,

00:16:59.143 --> 00:17:01.020
you know, faba bean,

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:04.148
and things that are readily
available in agriculture.

00:17:04.148 --> 00:17:06.900
The answer has entirely to do with

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.318
the scale of our business.

00:17:08.318 --> 00:17:11.238
So if you think about Tyson or JBS,

00:17:11.238 --> 00:17:13.782
you know, you look at how
much food they are producing.

00:17:13.782 --> 00:17:15.367
I heard at one point
that Tyson was touching

00:17:15.367 --> 00:17:17.786
two out of every five
plates in the United States.

00:17:17.786 --> 00:17:19.955
So they have a scale advantage over us

00:17:19.955 --> 00:17:22.416
and we have to continue
to scale our business.

00:17:22.416 --> 00:17:24.585
We have to continue to
mature our supply chain,

00:17:24.585 --> 00:17:26.462
enter more competition
into our supply chain

00:17:26.462 --> 00:17:28.672
so we can get lower cost inputs,

00:17:28.672 --> 00:17:30.007
but we're doing all that now.

00:17:30.007 --> 00:17:31.508
And we're on that cost curve.

00:17:31.508 --> 00:17:33.469
And so if you think about
the computer industry

00:17:33.469 --> 00:17:34.762
or the semiconductor industry,

00:17:34.762 --> 00:17:37.556
or any of these technologies come along,

00:17:37.556 --> 00:17:38.766
we are gonna make this cheaper

00:17:38.766 --> 00:17:39.975
and cheaper and cheaper to the point where

00:17:39.975 --> 00:17:41.685
we'll underprice animal protein.

00:17:41.685 --> 00:17:42.853
Why is that so important?

00:17:42.853 --> 00:17:44.813
For obvious reasons, but look,

00:17:44.813 --> 00:17:47.357
we'll get closer and closer

00:17:47.357 --> 00:17:50.110
to the animal protein that we're trying to

00:17:51.528 --> 00:17:53.614
provide an alternative to,

00:17:53.614 --> 00:17:54.990
in terms of the sensory experience,

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:56.742
the taste, the aroma, the appearance,

00:17:56.742 --> 00:17:59.912
all of that stuff will keep
getting better every year.

00:17:59.912 --> 00:18:02.748
Second, we'll get the
nutrition to the point where

00:18:02.748 --> 00:18:04.333
everyone understands that point, right.

00:18:04.333 --> 00:18:05.959
Right, now there's a lot
of noise about process

00:18:05.959 --> 00:18:06.919
and all this stuff.

00:18:06.919 --> 00:18:08.378
We're gonna do a lot of education about

00:18:08.378 --> 00:18:10.255
why this product is
healthier for you, right?

00:18:10.255 --> 00:18:12.216
So taste, health,

00:18:12.216 --> 00:18:14.051
and then you drop the
price below animal protein,

00:18:14.051 --> 00:18:16.136
it becomes a rare consumer that says,

00:18:16.136 --> 00:18:18.514
"This tastes just like animal protein.

00:18:18.514 --> 00:18:20.015
It's better for me. And it's cheaper.

00:18:20.015 --> 00:18:21.058
I'm still not gonna eat it."

00:18:21.058 --> 00:18:22.976
So we've gotta get those three things done

00:18:22.976 --> 00:18:25.437
and we've gotta get 'em
done in the next few years.

00:18:25.437 --> 00:18:27.439
Right, cause my next question was gonna be

00:18:27.439 --> 00:18:32.444
for me, it was somewhat
easy to convert me, right.

00:18:32.444 --> 00:18:35.906
Because the inflammation,
me being an athlete, right.

00:18:35.906 --> 00:18:38.700
But how do you convert
the diehard meat eaters?

00:18:39.618 --> 00:18:42.412
Independent of ideological boundaries

00:18:42.412 --> 00:18:45.624
or, you know, socioeconomic,

00:18:45.624 --> 00:18:48.710
all the divisions that
we have in our society.

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:52.047
You're still seeing people take this step

00:18:52.047 --> 00:18:54.341
and become largely flexitarian.

00:18:54.341 --> 00:18:57.719
And it's driven primarily by health.

00:18:57.719 --> 00:18:58.804
You know, it doesn't matter

00:18:58.804 --> 00:19:01.265
whether you're in a field in Idaho

00:19:01.265 --> 00:19:03.600
or in one of the densest
urban neighborhoods

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.727
in the United States, on the coast,

00:19:05.727 --> 00:19:08.063
or in Chicago, or what have you,

00:19:08.063 --> 00:19:11.525
if your doctor tells you
that you are going to die

00:19:11.525 --> 00:19:13.235
if you don't change something, right,

00:19:13.235 --> 00:19:14.528
you're gonna listen.

00:19:14.528 --> 00:19:15.863
And not everybody listens,

00:19:15.863 --> 00:19:17.865
but enough people are listening

00:19:17.865 --> 00:19:19.616
where they're breaking through

00:19:19.616 --> 00:19:21.869
and they're setting aside stereotypes

00:19:21.869 --> 00:19:24.288
and things that are not serving them.

00:19:24.288 --> 00:19:27.457
And so for us, health is
the number one message.

00:19:27.457 --> 00:19:28.750
And that differentiates us by the way,

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:30.627
from some of the companies
that are out there.

00:19:30.627 --> 00:19:32.713
You know, when we talk
about our ingredients,

00:19:32.713 --> 00:19:34.548
we say, you know, first of all,

00:19:34.548 --> 00:19:36.425
it wouldn't be responsible
to load our product up

00:19:36.425 --> 00:19:37.843
with high levels of saturated fat.

00:19:37.843 --> 00:19:40.429
So we're always gonna offer the consumer,

00:19:40.429 --> 00:19:42.222
if you look at our burger,

00:19:42.222 --> 00:19:46.059
significantly les saturated
fat than what's in beef, right.

00:19:46.059 --> 00:19:48.353
So we have, I think in
the Beyond Burger 3.0,

00:19:48.353 --> 00:19:53.066
we have 35% less saturated
fat in one of the versions

00:19:53.066 --> 00:19:55.319
and a second, which is
meant to be a lean burger,

00:19:55.319 --> 00:19:57.613
we have 55% less saturated fat.

00:19:57.613 --> 00:19:59.281
We have, you know, no GMOs.

00:19:59.281 --> 00:20:00.115
So there's no,

00:20:01.116 --> 00:20:02.492
you know, novel ingredients there.

00:20:02.492 --> 00:20:06.330
It's just, we're using
the plant kingdom in ways

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:08.582
that present it to the
human-centric experience

00:20:08.582 --> 00:20:09.917
as animal protein.

00:20:10.751 --> 00:20:12.544
And then no heterocyclic amines

00:20:12.544 --> 00:20:14.379
all of the things that are of concern

00:20:14.379 --> 00:20:16.131
within the medical community
about animal protein,

00:20:16.131 --> 00:20:18.634
we have kept out of our products, right.

00:20:18.634 --> 00:20:19.551
Or minimized.

00:20:19.551 --> 00:20:23.055
And so we have to keep
conveying to people,

00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:24.056
the health benefits of eating it.

00:20:24.056 --> 00:20:26.558
And that's the quickest
way to their plate.

00:20:27.935 --> 00:20:31.563
Last but not least, you know,

00:20:31.563 --> 00:20:34.024
everyone defines this differently.

00:20:34.024 --> 00:20:35.359
What is success to you?

00:20:35.359 --> 00:20:37.110
What is success look like to you

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:38.987
when it comes to you and your company?

00:20:39.947 --> 00:20:41.114
You know, it really,

00:20:42.616 --> 00:20:44.243
we're trying to do something.

00:20:44.243 --> 00:20:46.495
And when I say we, you
know, we're all in this,

00:20:46.495 --> 00:20:47.663
doing this together,

00:20:47.663 --> 00:20:51.375
to help, you know, save lives

00:20:51.375 --> 00:20:53.418
and help people live a healthier life.

00:20:53.418 --> 00:20:57.130
And to help repair our planet.

00:20:57.130 --> 00:21:00.259
Success for me is seeing
as many people as possible

00:21:00.259 --> 00:21:02.094
in the locations that they love to eat.

00:21:02.094 --> 00:21:04.137
And that's why I'm so focused
on places like McDonald's

00:21:04.137 --> 00:21:06.056
and KFC and others,

00:21:08.141 --> 00:21:10.018
enjoy themselves, enjoy the meal,

00:21:10.018 --> 00:21:11.645
have great tasting products,

00:21:11.645 --> 00:21:13.355
but do it in a way that's
better for their bodies

00:21:13.355 --> 00:21:15.440
and do it in a way that's
better for the earth.

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:17.943
And so that's really how
we do measure success here.

00:21:17.943 --> 00:21:18.777
It's a mission,

00:21:18.777 --> 00:21:21.488
you know, it's something
that's very personal.

00:21:21.488 --> 00:21:23.365
And, you know, we hope to look back

00:21:23.365 --> 00:21:25.951
and be that group of people
that separated meat from animals

00:21:25.951 --> 00:21:29.037
and brought the benefits
that that brings to

00:21:29.037 --> 00:21:31.748
not only humans, but the
rest of life on earth.

00:21:31.748 --> 00:21:34.376
Well, listen, Ethan, thank you so much.

00:21:34.376 --> 00:21:36.628
I appreciate you giving
us all this insight,

00:21:36.628 --> 00:21:37.796
all this wisdom.

00:21:37.796 --> 00:21:38.588
Thanks man.

